{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "latlon3d_diffusion_vertical_noflux",
    "description": "MMS driver: variable-coefficient VERTICAL TURBULENT DIFFUSION (Kzz / boundary-layer mixing) on the 3-D latlon3d grid, u_t = D(kz * D(u,lev), lev) + S, discretized by grids/latlon3d/rules/varcoeff_laplacian_lev_noflux_bc (conservative finite-volume d/dz(kz du/dz) on the non-uniform vertical, ZERO-FLUX at both the surface and the model top, rank-3). The vertical-mixing counterpart of the latlon3d advection drivers: together with problems/latlon3d_advection_vertical_ppm_mono it makes up the minimum viable chemical-transport COLUMN (transport + mixing), and the two rules compose in one model because their match patterns are structurally distinct (plain D(f,lev) for advection, the compound D(kz*D(u,lev),lev) at priority 10 for diffusion). PASSIVE lon (NLON = 8) and lat (NLAT = 5): the operator is purely vertical, so every column is identical and refinement is in NLEV alone. MANUFACTURED SOLUTION u*(z,t) = exp(-pi^2 t) cos(pi z), a decaying cosine. Its gradient u*_z = -pi exp(-pi^2 t) sin(pi z) VANISHES at z = 0 and z = 1, so the physical flux kz u*_z vanishes at both walls for ANY diffusivity and matches the zero-flux closure exactly, at every t. Because cos(pi z) is not an eigenfunction of the variable-coefficient operator a forcing S = u*_t - d/dz(kz du*/dz) is added so u* solves the continuous PDE exactly; with kz = 0.5(1 + 4z(1-z)) and kz' = 2(1 - 2z) it collapses to S(z,t) = exp(-pi^2 t) [ pi^2 (kz(z) - 1) cos(pi z) + pi kz'(z) sin(pi z) ], spelled below as the time factor exp(-pi^2 t) times a static aggregate over cell centres -- the same shape as problems/heat_1d_varcoeff_zero_flux. SECOND ORDER, verified 2.00 in BOTH L2 and Linf (see tests/conformance/convergence/latlon3d_diffusion_vertical_noflux). Note the operator's INTERIOR truncation error is O(dz^2) but its two boundary cells are O(dz): imposing the EXACT wall flux breaks the leading-order cancellation between neighbouring face-flux errors that the interior enjoys. The parabolic solve smooths that boundary-localised defect back to O(dz^2) in the SOLUTION (Gustafsson), which is what this test measures. Trading the exact wall flux away for a formally higher-order boundary closure would cost exact mass conservation -- a bad trade for a tracer-transport model, so it is not made. VERTICAL MESH: the consumer supplies pe (quadratic stretch, shape [lev_nodes], thin cells at the ground); the grid derives dz via lev_thickness and zc via lev_center. HYGIENE: pe over kn, kz over (ik,jk,kk), the forcing over (ia,ja,ka), dz/zc via gk -- all distinct from the operator's (i,j,k). ic/references spell z inline from the stretch formula and the NLEV name (build-time scope caveat).",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "op:varcoeff_laplacian",
      "axes:lev",
      "bc:no-flux",
      "family:finite_volume"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Jacobson, M. Z. (2005). Fundamentals of Atmospheric Modeling (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press. Section 8.4 (vertical turbulent diffusion of a tracer)."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Roache, P. J. (2002). Code verification by the method of manufactured solutions. Journal of Fluids Engineering, 124(1), 4-10.",
        "doi": "10.1115/1.1436090"
      }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "VerticalDiffusion3d": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/varcoeff_laplacian_lev_noflux_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 8,
            "NLAT": 5
          }
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "u": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Vertically mixed tracer over lon x lat x lev (horizontally uniform here). Cell-centre values on the stretched vertical mesh."
        },
        "pe": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Level-edge coordinates of the quadratic-stretch vertical mesh, pe[kn] = 0.6 s + 0.4 s^2 with s = (kn-1)/NLEV, over node kn = 1..NLEV+1 (pe[1] = 0 at the ground, pe[NLEV+1] = 1 at the model top; monotone increasing since dpe/ds = 0.6 + 0.8 s > 0). Cells are THIN near the surface and thicken upward, which is the physically right stretch for a boundary-layer-resolving column. Identical to the mesh of problems/latlon3d_advection_vertical_ppm, so the advection and diffusion operators are exercised on the same vertical grid. HYGIENE: aggregates over the node index `kn` (not k), so inlined pe[k]/dz[k]/zc[k] reads do not capture a consuming vertical stencil's own aggregate index k."
        },
        "dz": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Cell thicknesses dz[k] = pe[k+1] - pe[k], derived by the grid's lev_thickness template (which aggregates over the geometry index gk). This is the `dz` free name read by the diffusion rule's stencils."
        },
        "zc": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Cell centres zc[k] = (pe[k] + pe[k+1])/2, derived by the grid's lev_center template (aggregating over the geometry index gk). Read here only to build the diffusivity kz and the manufactured forcing; the diffusion stencils themselves need only `dz`."
        },
        "kz": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Vertical turbulent diffusivity Kzz(z) = 0.5 (1 + 4 z (1 - z)), a parabolic boundary-layer mixing profile: it vanishes nowhere on [0,1] (range [0.5, 1.0]), peaks at mid-column and falls to 0.5 at the ground and the lid, which is the classic O'Brien/Troen-Mahrt shape of a well-mixed PBL. Horizontally uniform here (the vertical operator is what is under test), but declared with the full [lon,lat,lev] shape the rule's match requires so a real consumer can supply a genuinely 3-D Kzz. Bound by the rule's match as the coefficient kz -- the LEFT factor of kz * D(u, lev). HYGIENE: aggregates over (ik, jk, kk), all distinct from the operator's (i, j, k)."
        }
      },
